Here's my analysis of `InputValidatorTests.cs`:

---

## Summary

**1 major finding** — your 9 test methods are classic copy-paste tests that differ only in input and expected output. This is a textbook candidate for **parameterized tests** using `[DataRow]`.

---

## Finding: Copy-Paste Test Methods (Category 3)

Every method follows the same pattern:

```csharp
var validator = new InputValidator();
var result = validator.Validate(INPUT);
Assert.IsTrue/IsFalse(result.IsValid);
Assert.AreEqual/IsNull(result.ErrorMessage);
```

### Suggested refactoring — use `[DataTestMethod]` + `[DataRow]`:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class InputValidatorTests
{
    [DataTestMethod]
    [DataRow("", false, "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Empty string")]
    [DataRow(null, false, "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Null")]
    [DataRow("   ", false, "Input cannot be empty", DisplayName = "Whitespace only")]
    [DataRow("ab", false, "Input must be at least 3 characters", DisplayName = "Too short")]
    [DataRow("hello<script>", false, "Input contains invalid characters", DisplayName = "Special chars")]
    [DataRow("hello-world", true, null, DisplayName = "Valid input")]
    [DataRow("abc", true, null, DisplayName = "Min length boundary")]
    public void Validate_ReturnsExpectedResult(string? input, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }
}
```

For the "too long" and "max length" cases (which use `new string(...)` — not a compile-time constant), use `[DynamicData]`:

```csharp
    [DataTestMethod]
    [DynamicData(nameof(LengthBoundaryCases), DynamicDataSourceType.Method)]
    public void Validate_LengthBoundaries_ReturnsExpectedResult(string input, bool expectedValid, string? expectedError)
    {
        var validator = new InputValidator();
        var result = validator.Validate(input);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedValid, result.IsValid);
        Assert.AreEqual(expectedError, result.ErrorMessage);
    }

    private static IEnumerable<object?[]> LengthBoundaryCases()
    {
        yield return new object?[] { new string('x', 256), false, "Input must not exceed 255 characters" };
        yield return new object?[] { new string('a', 255), true, null };
    }
```

---

## Impact

| Metric | Before | After |
|--------|--------|-------|
| Test methods | 9 | 2 |
| Lines of code | ~80 | ~35 |
| Adding a new case | New method (~6 lines) | One `[DataRow]` line |

## Trade-offs

- **Gained:** Adding a test case is now a single `[DataRow]` attribute — no new method needed.
- **Lost:** Individual methods are slightly easier to debug in isolation (though `DisplayName` provides equivalent traceability in test output).
- **Verdict:** Strongly worth it at 9 occurrences of the same pattern.

Would you like me to apply this refactoring to the file?
